      "dom_id": "question-109537",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find, in point-slope form, the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-3,-1)$ and $(4,2).$",
      "worked_solution": "We know that the line passes through the point $(-3,-1),$ so we can use $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-3,-1)$ in our point-slope formula. All that remains is to compute $m,$ and we can do this using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{2-(-1)}{4-(-3)}=\\frac{2+1}{4+3}=\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=\\frac{3}{7}$ and the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-3,-1)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =\\frac{3}{7}(x-(-3)) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{3}{7}(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
